 1 big thing: Month that changed the market 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Data: Financial Modeling Prep . Chart: Erin Davis/Axios As stocks limp to the end of a terrible, horrible, no good, very bad month, the AI trade is in uncharted and highly uncertain new territory.

 Why it matters: AI exuberance has been driving markets and the economy for the past several years — so it's difficult to overstate the change in the investment environment over just the last few weeks, Axios' Emily Peck and Matt Phillips write. 😨 By the numbers: The tech-heavy Nasdaq is down 11.1% since Nov. 1. Both the Nasdaq and the S&amp;P 500, the benchmark stock index, are now in correction territory — meaning down more than 10% from their most recent highs.

 The closely watched SOX semiconductor index, which tracks many of the high-flying chip stocks that supercharged markets, is down 21.2% over that same period. Nvidia, the premier stock of the AI trade, is down a shocking 38.4% this month. The stock closed at $125.53 yesterday. In May, it was trading at more than $220. Lead stories of today's New York Times and Wall Street Journal. Catch   up quick: AI fever was already starting to break heading into November, and then three major headwinds appear to have crushed the vibes.

 🗳️ Americans voted overwhelmingly for AI-skeptical candidates and ballot measures constraining data center construction. 👀 Nvidia CEO Jensen Huang, who helped fuel the AI infrastructure boom by extending a range of unorthodox financial lifelines to smaller, heavily indebted AI players, resigned amid scandal. 🤖 AI lab Anthropic listed its shares in the biggest offering of all time. The widely watched milestone failed to lift investor sentiment on tech, and shares continued to tumble. Get Axios Markets free each morning . 

 
 
