 2. 🏛️ Congress gets a huge makeover 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Data: House historian, U.S. Senate . Chart: Erin Davis/Axios Americans want change, and they're about to get it: A perfect storm of congressional retirements, primary upsets, gerrymandering, flipped seats and lawmakers who died in office has created the biggest freshman class in decades, Axios' Justin Green and Kathleen Hunter write. 

 Why it matters: Congress had a 14% approval rating (not a typo) when Gallup polled registered voters in October. That's its lowest pre-midterm number since 1974. The big picture: The Senate is on track for its biggest freshman class since 1946. In the House, it's shaping up to be the biggest since 1992.

 Congress is saying goodbye to former House Majority Leader Steny Hoyer (D-Md.), Senate Minority Whip Dick Durbin (D-Ill.), former Senate GOP leader Mitch McConnell (R-Ky.) and former Speaker Nancy Pelosi (D-Calif.). They've been in Congress since 1981, 1983, 1985 and 1987, respectively. Share this story . 

 
 
