 5. ⛽ Driving through the pain 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 High gas prices swayed voters, but haven't kept Americans off the roads.

 Why it matters: Most driving isn't especially optional. So people are just absorbing the cost, Axios' Ben Geman reports. By the numbers: The Energy Policy Research Foundation finds that American households spent $300 to $730 more on gas from March through August than during the same period last year.

 But there's "no discernible evidence of a decline in gasoline consumption," UC Berkeley energy economist Lucas Davis writes . "Drivers may talk about cutting back, but studies in the economics literature consistently find that gasoline demand is inelastic in the short run." Share this story . 

 
 
