 6. 🦾 Anthropic savant says he solved math&#x27;s most famous problem 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 Image via Levent Alpöge

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 A 34-year-old mathematician at Anthropic says he used Claude to solve the 167-year-old Holy Grail of math problems, the Riemann Hypothesis . 

 Why it matters: "For months now, mathematicians have been repeatedly stunned to see machines conquer mathematical problems that have eluded human geniuses for years — in the case of the Riemann Hypothesis, for more than a century and a half," the N.Y. Times reports ( gift link ). Levent Alpöge — a Turkish-American and native of Long Island who's a member of Anthropic's technical staff — made the claim with a four-page explanation posted on his website yesterday: 

 "Claude and I have had a great time together with such breadth in recent months, but this time Claude far outpaced me near the finish line. No, Claude flew, and danced, and sparkled near that finish line. ... Claude began to leave me in awe." Formulated in 1859, the Riemann Hypothesis centers on the distribution of prime numbers — those that are only divisible by one and themselves — as they grow toward infinity, AP reports . 

 A day after Anthropic's initial public offering — the most valuable in history — Alpöge revealed he'd solved the elusive problem. Alpöge released his and Claude's proof of the Riemann Hypothesis within 17 million lines of Lean code, a programming language that allows people to program proofs of mathematical theorems.

 Formally assessing whether the problem has actually been solved will take time. Just 10 weeks ago, OpenAI  said  its technology took 88 hours to crack the Navier-Stokes equations, which date to the 19th century. The bottom line: Scientific American says the AI-generated solution to the Riemann Hypothesis, "long considered far out of reach to human mathematicians, would cap off the technology's year-long conquest of math in spectacular fashion."

 See the four-page paper . 
 
