 8. 🍼 1 for the road: Bots name babies 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 Illustration: Maura Losch/Axios

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 AI may have ruined the name Claude. But now parents are turning to it for help naming their babies.

 Case in point: Baby-naming website Nameberry is already feeling the effects of AI. The site's users and pageviews both fell roughly 40% between October 2025 and October 2026, according to Nameberry co-creator and CEO Pam Redmond. Chatbots are good at suggesting names, Redmond says. But she suspects they could steer more parents toward the same trendy, "middlebrow cool" choices like Sloane and Sutton. 🔮 What's next: Names gaining popularity, per Nameberry's internal search data, include Beatrice, Sabine, Fiona, Ivy, Mae, Marin, Gwendolyn, Murray and Conrad.

 Others Redmond predicts will take off : Rosemary, Mabel, Archie and Bodie. By sharing these names, we may have just given the bots a few more to suggest.

